insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('044b491b-0681-5dc8-9385-98b6837d00fe', 'rg8-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Noise-Canceling Headphones Work$q$, $q$On a long bus ride, the steady rumble of the engine can make it hard to hear music or even think. Noise-canceling headphones are designed to solve that problem. Surprisingly, they fight sound by making more sound.

To understand how, it helps to picture sound as a wave. When an engine rumbles, it pushes air molecules together and then lets them spread apart, over and over. These squeezed and stretched regions travel to your ear as a wave with peaks and valleys.

Each earcup contains a tiny microphone that listens to the noise outside. A small computer chip studies the incoming wave and quickly creates a new wave that is its mirror image. Where the noise has a peak, the new wave has a valley. The speaker plays this mirror wave along with your music.

When the two waves meet, they cancel each other out. Imagine one person pushing a door while another person pushes back with equal force on the other side. The door does not move. In the same way, the air near your ear barely moves, so the rumble seems to disappear.

The method works best on steady, low sounds such as engines and fans. Sudden or high sounds, like a voice or a dog's bark, change too quickly for the chip to match perfectly. That is why many headphones also use thick cushions to block those sounds the old-fashioned way.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
