-- Passage rg8-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0a6cc4f9-ec63-52d2-8eba-a2e0b9293f82', 'rg8-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Return of the Beavers$q$, $q$For nearly a century, no beavers lived along Copper Creek. Trappers had removed them long ago, and without their dams, the creek ran fast and straight through a narrow channel. In dry summers, parts of it shrank to a trickle, and the meadows beside it turned brown.

Then, eight years ago, wildlife biologists released six beaver families upstream. Within a single season, the animals began building dams of branches and mud. Each dam slowed the water and spread it into shallow ponds.

Those ponds set off a series of changes. Slower water let mud and sand settle, which made the water clearer. Water also soaked into the ground instead of rushing away, so the creek kept flowing even in August. The damp soil allowed willows and sedges to grow along the banks.

The new plants, in turn, attracted other animals. Songbirds nested in the willows. Frogs laid eggs in the calm ponds. Trout found cool, deep pools to rest in during hot afternoons.

Not everyone welcomed the change at first. A few landowners worried that the ponds would flood their roads. The biologists worked with them to install pipes through certain dams, keeping water at a safe level. Today, Copper Creek is wider, slower, and greener, and it is full of life that had been missing for generations.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
