-- Passage rg9-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d76e6eab-a516-5fac-a383-59c5b56b08a6', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why the Ice Trade Melted$q$, $q$For about seventy years, the town of Halvering earned its living from frozen water. Each winter, workers cut blocks of ice from Halvering Lake, packed them in sawdust, and shipped them by rail to cities as far as three hundred miles away. By 1925, the business had nearly vanished. Many local histories say that electric refrigerators destroyed it. The records suggest a more complicated story.

Electric refrigerators did matter, but they arrived late. Sales records from the Halvering hardware store show that fewer than one home in twenty in the region owned one in 1925. A product found in so few homes cannot by itself explain a collapse that was already nearly complete.

Something else was happening on the lake. Beginning around 1905, a paper mill upstream released waste into the water. Letters kept by the Halvering Ice Company show that city buyers rejected whole shipments after 1910 because the ice was cloudy and smelled bad. The company's sales fell by half between 1910 and 1916.

Weather added to the trouble. A diary kept by the ice company's foreman records three winters between 1912 and 1919 when the lake never froze thick enough to cut safely. In those years, the company had nothing to ship at all.

Meanwhile, factories in the cities had begun making ice with machines. This factory ice was clean, available in any season, and did not need to travel by train.

The refrigerator was the final blow to an industry that was already failing. To understand why Halvering's ice trade ended, we have to look at the lake, the weather and the new factories, not only at the kitchen.$q$, 'draft')
