   $q$Why might the sign-up sheet overstate how many students would actually play?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Students who sign up for clubs never join school sports.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"Sixty-two students is too few to form even one team.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The student council cannot legally run a sign-up sheet.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Signing a sheet takes little effort and does not promise attendance.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Signing a sheet takes little effort and does not promise attendance.$q$, $q$Writing a name on a cafeteria sheet is easy, so some signers may not show up for practices all season. The passage says sixty-two is "more than enough for two full teams," so the number is not too small.$q$, 'draft', 'db35ec01-9983-543a-bc87-aa8201eaffdf')
