-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f573efa4-49a6-59e0-a30b-8a3e8ced985b', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Every Student Should Learn to Swim$q$, $q$From a column by Delphine Achterberg, aquatics director at Harborview High School, written for the school's newsletter to families.

Every fall, I ask my new ninth graders a simple question: who here could swim the length of this pool without stopping? Every year, a few hands stay down. Those students are not lazy or careless. Most of them simply never had the chance to learn, because lessons cost money, the nearest pool was across town, or no adult in their lives knew how to teach them.

I understand why a family might think swimming is optional. It is not on any state test, and many of our students live far from a beach. But water is everywhere in ordinary life. There are creeks behind neighborhoods, lakes at summer camps, and flooded streets after heavy storms. A teenager who cannot swim is not safer for staying away from water. That teenager is simply less prepared when water shows up unexpectedly.

Swimming is also one of the few sports that people keep doing for their whole lives. I have watched students who arrived terrified of the deep end leave in June swimming laps with confidence. One of them told me that learning to float was the first time she ever felt her body was on her side. That kind of confidence carries over to the classroom, the stage, and the playing field.

Our school already has a pool. What we lack is time in the schedule. I am asking families to support a proposal, going to the school board next month, that would add a required six-week swim unit to ninth-grade physical education. Students who already swim well could test out and help coach others.

No student should leave our building unable to keep themselves safe in the water. We have the pool, the coaches, and the will. All we need now is your voice.$q$, 'draft')
