-- Passage rg9-authors-purpose-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d71593b3-00b0-5b53-bccd-0c56475dc2c8', 'rg9-authors-purpose-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Long Beats Strange$q$, $q$From a guide written by Oluwaseun Pereira, technology coordinator at Fairmont Regional High School, for students setting up their new school accounts.

For years, you have probably been told that a strong password looks like a cat walked across a keyboard. It needs a capital letter, a number, a symbol, and nothing a person could ever remember. I am here to tell you that this advice, while well meant, has led many of us in the wrong direction.

Here is the problem. When a password is hard to remember, people find ways around it. They write it on a sticky note attached to their screen. They use the same one for every account. They change it by adding a 1, then a 2, then a 3 at the end. Each of these habits makes an account easier to break into, not harder.

What actually makes a password strong is mostly length. Computer programs that guess passwords work by trying enormous numbers of combinations, and every extra character multiplies the number of possibilities they must try. A string of four ordinary but unrelated words, such as a phrase about a purple ladder and a sleepy trumpet, is far longer than a short jumble of symbols. It is also far easier for you to remember.

That does not mean any phrase will do. Avoid song lyrics, famous sayings, and anything about yourself that a classmate could guess, such as your pet's name or your jersey number. The words should be random enough that no one could connect them to you.

Finally, use a different passphrase for every important account. If one website is ever broken into, your other accounts will stay safe.

You will be creating your new school password this week. Take five minutes to make it long, odd, and memorable. Your future self, who will not be locked out of the grade portal at midnight, will thank you.$q$, 'draft')
