-- Passage rg9-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('85aec348-c6e8-5c38-aaf7-533aef6db3a2', 'rg9-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Half an Inch$q$, $q$On Saturdays Priya Raman worked the back room of her aunt Meera's alterations shop, a narrow space that smelled of steam and machine oil. Aunt Meera handled the customers and the difficult fabrics. Priya handled hems, buttons, and whatever her aunt called "honest work for beginners."

That morning the job was a gray suit belonging to Mr. Delacroix, a quiet man who had told Aunt Meera he had a job interview on Monday. The pinned trousers hung on the rack with a tag in her aunt's neat handwriting: Take up 1 1/2 inches.

Priya measured, chalked, cut, and stitched. She was proud of how fast she worked. Only when she pressed the second leg did she see the tag again and notice the faint line under the one. She had read it as two and a half inches. The trousers were a full inch too short, and the fabric she had trimmed away was lying in the scrap bin.

For a long minute she stood at the ironing board. Aunt Meera was busy at the front counter. The suit was due at four. Priya thought about letting the hems back down as far as they would go and saying nothing; maybe Mr. Delacroix would not notice, or would think he had stood wrong at the fitting.

Instead she carried the trousers to the counter. "I cut these wrong," she said. "I misread the tag. It is my fault, not the pattern's."

Aunt Meera unfolded the leg, studied the hem, and was silent long enough that Priya's face burned. Then she reached under the counter for a roll of matching cloth. "We can add a facing inside," she said. "It is slow work. You will do it, and I will check every stitch."

Priya worked through lunch. At four, Mr. Delacroix tried on the suit and smiled at the mirror. When he left, Aunt Meera moved the tag rack from the back room to the front, beside her own table.

"From now on," she said, "you read the tags out loud to me first."$q$, 'draft')
