-- Passage rg9-character-setting-plot-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('32fd7b18-dd14-5b66-8d69-85c3851a23ba', 'rg9-character-setting-plot-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Silent Clock$q$, $q$The regional chess championship was held in a middle school cafeteria, and by the final round the room was so quiet that Kwame Asante could hear the refrigerator humming behind the serving line. Thirty boards stood in rows on folding tables. At board one, Kwame faced Hana Sato, the defending champion, who had beaten him twice the year before.

This time he was winning, or nearly. Hana had made a risky trade in the middle game, and now her king stood exposed. She leaned over the board with her chin on her fists, thinking hard, and her clock ticked down past four minutes, then three.

Then Kwame noticed something. When Hana made her last move, she had tapped the button on her side of the clock too lightly. Her time was still running, but so was his. The clock was broken, or at least confused, and both displays were draining together.

Kwame knew the rule. Either player could stop the clock and call the tournament director. He also knew that if he said nothing, Hana's time would probably run out first, and the trophy would be his without another move. His coach, Mr. Brandt, was watching from the doorway with his arms folded.

Kwame glanced at his own display, then at hers. He thought of the year before, when he had lost to her on a fair board and walked out to the parking lot angry for a full hour. He did not want to win the way he had lost: without understanding how.

He pressed the pause button and raised his hand. The director came, checked the clock, replaced it, and restored the correct times. Hana looked at Kwame for a moment as if she were seeing him for the first time.

The game lasted forty more minutes. Kwame won on the board, with a quiet rook move that left her king nowhere to go. When they shook hands, Hana held on a second longer than usual. "Rematch in the spring," she said, and it did not sound like a threat.$q$, 'draft')
