   $q$Why does the memory of last year's loss matter to Kwame's decision?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It makes him want revenge so badly that he is willing to win in any way he can manage.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"It makes him so nervous about losing that he stops the clock to calm himself down first.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It reminds him he wants a win he earns and understands, not one handed to him by a broken clock.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It reminds him that Hana cheated him last year, so he wants to catch her cheating again.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It reminds him he wants a win he earns and understands, not one handed to him by a broken clock.$q$, $q$The text says he "did not want to win the way he had lost: without understanding how," so the memory pushes him toward a fair result. Last year's board was "fair," so nothing suggests Hana cheated.$q$, 'draft', '32fd7b18-dd14-5b66-8d69-85c3851a23ba')
