-- Passage rg9-character-setting-plot-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f602dc38-6f57-521f-9030-c7c6764339da', 'rg9-character-setting-plot-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Magpie and the Clock Tower Owl$q$, $q$In the square of a hill town stood a clock tower, and in the top of the tower lived an old owl named Ormond. Every night he sat beside the great brass gears and listened. If a gear slipped, he heard it. If the pendulum dragged, he nudged it. Because of Ormond, the clock kept perfect time, and the town woke, worked, and slept by its bells.

A young magpie named Pell admired the tower for a different reason. The gears shone. Through a gap in the stones she could see small brass pins and washers glittering in the moonlight, and nothing in her nest was half so bright.

One night, while Ormond dozed on the far ledge, Pell slipped in and pulled out a single small pin. The gear beside it gave a little shiver but kept turning. Pell flew home delighted. Surely, she thought, one pin was nothing to a clock so large.

The next night she took another. The night after that, two. Each time the clock kept going, and each time she grew bolder.

Then one morning the bells rang at the wrong hour. The baker opened before his bread was risen. The children walked to school in the dark. Farmers arrived at an empty market and stood confused in the square.

Ormond searched the gears until he found the gaps. He did not shout. He flew to Pell's nest, where the pins lay arranged in a bright circle, and he looked at them for a long time.

"They are beautiful," he said at last. "They were also holding up the morning."

Pell looked down at the confused farmers in the square. She carried the pins back one by one, and for a month afterward she sat beside Ormond each night, learning to hear when a gear began to slip.

A small theft can stop a great machine.$q$, 'draft')
