-- Passage rg9-character-setting-plot-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('be92127d-d0c9-54f5-80f6-b8ebc41372f7', 'rg9-character-setting-plot-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Proof$q$, $q$In the spring of 1924, the Millbrook Courier was printed in a brick building beside the railroad tracks, and every Thursday night the floor shook with the old flatbed press. Esther Lindqvist, fifteen, had been an apprentice there since winter. She swept lead shavings, inked the rollers, and read proof sheets for the editor, Mr. Halvorsen, who trusted no one's eyes but his own.

That Thursday the front page announced the winner of the county spelling bee. The story had been set in type late, because Mr. Halvorsen had written it himself after a long day. At ten o'clock he handed Esther the proof, said "Quickly," and went back to the press.

Esther read the headline twice. It named the winner as Ruth Pellerin. But Esther had been in the town hall that afternoon, and she had watched Ruth miss the word "rhythm" in the final round. The winner was Clara Adeyemi, a quiet girl from the farm road who had spelled it without a pause.

The press was already running. Each sheet that slid off the roller carried the wrong name. Esther knew that Mr. Halvorsen hated interruptions more than he hated mistakes, and she had never once stopped the press.

She walked up to him anyway, holding the proof flat so he could see it. "The winner was Clara," she said. "I was there. Ruth missed the last word."

Mr. Halvorsen frowned at her, then at the page. For a long moment only the press spoke. Then he pulled the lever, and the great machine sighed to a stop.

They reset the headline by lamplight. It took until nearly two in the morning, and they threw away three hundred spoiled sheets. Mr. Halvorsen said very little. But the next week, when the new proof came off the press, he handed it to Esther first and waited beside her while she read.$q$, 'draft')
