-- Passage rg9-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5122349c-7fe9-50ca-b890-90568e87b6b9', 'rg9-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Quarry Layer$q$, $q$The summer program at the limestone quarry ran for three weeks, and by the second Monday Zainab Haddad had learned two things. Fossil hunting was mostly kneeling in the sun with a brush. And Rowan Fitch, who had been coming to the quarry since he was ten, talked as if he had already found everything worth finding.

The group was working a gray shelf of rock that Dr. Okafor, the paleontologist in charge, called the shell layer. Most of what they found were small clams pressed flat in the stone, millions of years old and very common. Rowan sorted them quickly into trays. Anything odd he tossed onto the reject pile with a flick of his wrist.

Zainab was sifting through that pile, mostly from boredom, when she noticed a fragment that did not curve like a shell. It had a row of tiny ridges along one edge, like the teeth of a comb. She held it up to Rowan.

"Broken clam," he said without looking.

Zainab did not argue. But she did not put it down, either. She took out her notebook and drew it carefully, measuring the ridges with a ruler. That evening she compared her sketch to the field guides in the trailer, page by page, until the light outside turned orange.

The next morning she showed Dr. Okafor the fragment and the drawing together. Dr. Okafor turned the piece under a hand lens for a long time. Then she said it was part of a jaw from a small fish, the first one anyone had found in the shell layer.

Rowan stood behind them with his hands in his pockets. Zainab could have said nothing more. Instead she turned and asked him where on the shelf the reject pile had come from, since he had worked there longest and would remember.

He did remember. By afternoon the two of them were kneeling side by side at that spot, brushing slowly, and Rowan was no longer flicking anything away.$q$, 'draft')
