-- Passage rg9-comparing-texts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6c3f7075-1d3b-539e-abd6-d3486257f75b', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Honest Scale$q$, $q$Text 1: The Salt Merchant's Scale (an invented parable)

In a market town beside the sea, a salt merchant named Orvel owned a brass scale that leaned slightly to one side. He knew about the lean, and he liked it, because every sack he weighed looked a little heavier than it really was. For a season his coins piled up. Then a fisherman's daughter weighed her salt again at home and found it short. She told her neighbors, and they told theirs. By winter, Orvel sat alone behind his crooked scale while the honest seller across the square could not keep her shelves full. A scale that cheats others also weighs the one who uses it.

Text 2: The Bent Rim (a story that retells the parable)

Every Saturday, Dalia Haddad sold repaired bicycles at the flea market with money she was saving for a used laptop. One morning a boy about eleven years old circled a blue bike three times before he asked the price. Dalia knew the back rim was slightly bent. If she spun the wheel slowly, nobody would notice the wobble.

She thought about how much faster she could reach her goal. Then she thought about the boy riding home, the wheel rubbing the brake pad until it wore through. She spun the wheel quickly instead, and the wobble showed. "It needs a new rim," she said. "I can swap it by next week, or you can take it today for less."

The boy's father, who had been watching from the next table, came over and shook her hand. The following Saturday he returned with two neighbors who also needed bikes. Dalia sold more that month than in any month before, but what she remembered most was the boy's grin when he rode off on a straight wheel.$q$, 'draft')
