   $q$Text 2 ends with what Dalia remembered most, not with her sales. What does this ending add to the parable's idea?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It suggests that the parable was wrong, because cheating sellers in Text 2 are never discovered.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It suggests that Dalia regrets losing the higher price she could have charged for the blue bicycle.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It suggests honesty is worth more than the profit it brings, since the reward she values is the boy's safe ride.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It suggests honesty is only worth practicing when a seller is sure that it will increase her profits.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It suggests honesty is worth more than the profit it brings, since the reward she values is the boy's safe ride.$q$, $q$Text 1 measures honesty by its effect on business, but Text 2 says Dalia sold more yet "remembered most" the boy's grin, adding a value beyond profit. Nothing shows regret about the lower price, so that choice is unsupported.$q$, 'draft', '6c3f7075-1d3b-539e-abd6-d3486257f75b')
