values ('cc3fe6f9-1c9a-5c29-801d-7ab56ebf3685', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Quiet Beat$q$, $q$Text 1: The Smallest Drum (an invented folk tale)

In a river village famous for its harvest festival, the drummers competed each year to play the loudest drum. The winner carried a drum as wide as a cart wheel, and crowds cheered whenever it boomed. At the back of the procession walked a girl named Suri with a drum no bigger than a cooking pot. No one cheered for her.

One year a storm of wind blew across the square, and the big drummers lost their places in the music, each pounding at a different speed. Dancers stumbled into one another. Then, under all the noise, the villagers heard a small, steady tap: Suri, still keeping time. One by one the drummers found her beat and followed it. After that festival, the smallest drum always led the procession.

Text 2: Stage Left (a story that retells the tale)

On opening night of the spring musical, the audience watched the actors. Nobody watched Mateo Lindqvist, crouched in the dark at stage left with a headset and a clipboard. His job was to call every lighting and scenery change at exactly the right moment.

Halfway through the second act, the sound system crackled and the actors' microphones cut out. The lead actor froze in the middle of a line, and the orchestra lost its place. Mateo kept his eyes on his script and spoke calmly into the headset: "Lights, cue forty-two. Fly crew, cue forty-three." The stage glowed blue, a painted moon slid down, and the actors, seeing the familiar change, found their places again.

At the cast party, the director raised a glass of lemonade to "the person you never saw." Mateo turned red. He had not wanted applause, but he kept the program with his name in small print at the bottom.$q$, 'draft')
