-- Passage rg9-connections-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('31a42254-5d03-5efd-85b2-b620d94fb34a', 'rg9-connections-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Library on Wheels$q$, $q$In 1934, Carrow County had one public library, and it sat in the county seat of Millbrook. For families on farms twenty or thirty miles away, the trip took most of a day by wagon or old truck. As a result, many children in the county's outer valleys grew up with no books at home except a calendar and an almanac.

The head librarian, Inés Abernathy, decided that if readers could not come to the library, the library would go to them. She persuaded a local lumber company to donate an old delivery truck. Volunteers built wooden shelves inside it and painted the words Carrow County Library on the sides.

The first trips were rough. The roads in the outer valleys were mostly dirt, and after rain the truck often became stuck in the mud. Abernathy responded by changing the schedule. The truck visited each stop once every two weeks, and in wet months it carried extra boxes of books so that families could borrow enough to last until the roads dried.

The effect on schools was soon noticed. Teachers in one-room schoolhouses reported that students who had once read only their single shared textbook were now reading novels, travel books and science guides. Several teachers began planning lessons around the truck's visits, asking students to report on the books they had borrowed.

By 1940, the county had added a second truck, and the number of library cards in the outer valleys had grown from fewer than fifty to more than nine hundred. When the county finally built branch libraries in the 1950s, it placed them in the towns where the trucks had drawn the most readers. The routes Abernathy designed had quietly become a map of where the county's readers lived.$q$, 'draft')
