-- Passage rg9-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('40f924f4-f253-57d7-98fa-eda8f4b547fd', 'rg9-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Fixing the Lunch Line$q$, $q$At Ridgeview Hollow High School, lunch lasts thirty minutes. Two years ago, students at the end of the line often waited nineteen minutes for their food, which left them about ten minutes to eat. Many simply skipped lunch, and teachers noticed that afternoon classes were full of tired, distracted students.

A group of students in a statistics class decided to study the problem. For two weeks, they stood near the cafeteria with stopwatches and timed each step of the line. They expected the slowest step to be the serving counter. Instead, they found that the counter moved fairly quickly. The real delay came at the end, where every student stopped at a single register to type in a lunch account number.

The students presented their findings to the principal, Mr. Haruki Bellweather, and the cafeteria manager. They suggested two changes. First, a second register would split the final step into two lines. Second, students could scan a card instead of typing a number. The cafeteria agreed to test both ideas for a month.

The results came quickly. With two registers and card scanning, the longest wait dropped from nineteen minutes to seven. Because students had more time, the number who bought lunch rose by nearly a third. The cafeteria manager also reported that less food was thrown away, since meals were no longer sitting under heat lamps while students waited.

Teachers noticed a change as well. Several said that students seemed more focused after lunch. The statistics class now tells each new group of students the story of the lunch line as an example of why it pays to measure a problem before guessing at its cause.$q$, 'draft')
