   $q$The word "true" usually means "correct" or "real." In paragraph 4, what does it mean for a wheel to be "trued"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"replaced with a newer and stronger wheel","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"adjusted so that it spins straight without wobbling","is_correct":true},{"text":"cleaned so that the metal shines again","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-context-clue"},{"text":"proven to be the original wheel from the bicycle","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"common-meaning-instead"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$adjusted so that it spins straight without wobbling$q$, $q$Grandpa Teodor explains that adjusting the spokes pulls the rim "back into a straight, even circle" so it spins "without wobbling," which is this special meaning of trued. Choosing "the original wheel" uses the everyday meaning of true as real, which does not fit a wobbling wheel.$q$, 'draft', '30314256-f0dd-5ce9-843b-ee6ddf9900fe')
