-- Passage rg9-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fcbdb092-21f2-56a5-bc50-13f4d8433832', 'rg9-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Stairs Made of Water$q$, $q$Water does not flow uphill, and neither can a loaded barge. Long ago, this was a serious problem for people who wanted to move goods by canal, because land is rarely flat for very long. Where the ground rose, early canal builders sometimes dragged boats up slopes on wooden rollers. The work was laborious, demanding dozens of workers and many hours of straining effort to move a single boat a short distance.

The solution was the canal lock, a device that works like a staircase made of water. A lock is a narrow stone or concrete chamber with heavy gates at each end. A vessel, whether a small rowboat or a long cargo barge, enters the chamber, and the gate behind it closes.

Next, workers open small valves that let water flow into the chamber from the higher section of the canal. The water level inside slowly rises, lifting the boat with it. When the water inside the lock reaches the same height as the water beyond the upper gate, the levels are said to equalize. At that point the gate swings open easily, because the water presses on both sides of it with the same force. The boat floats out onto the higher stretch of canal. To go down, the process simply runs in reverse.

The design is ingenious. It needs no engines and very little energy, only gravity and the water already in the canal, yet it can raise a heavy boat the height of a two-story house.

Locks also made travel safer. Before they existed, boats sometimes had to pass through rapids, where a single wrong turn could tip a barge and its cargo into the river. Compared with that precarious journey, the slow, steady rise inside a lock was calm and predictable.$q$, 'draft')
