   $q$Which phrase from the passage best helps a reader understand the word "trajectory"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"drags a thin layer of air around with it\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-context-clue"},{"text":"\"follows a bending path through the air\"","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"from the pitcher's hand to the plate\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"\"like a car steering gently\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-context-clue"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"follows a bending path through the air"$q$, $q$A trajectory is the path an object follows as it moves, and "follows a bending path through the air" restates that idea. The phrase "from the pitcher's hand to the plate" names only the start and end points, not the path itself.$q$, 'draft', '9e11bf80-ff75-501a-aecf-1443ffd0a4a5')
