-- Passage rg9-context-clues-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c66b1d7a-adef-5ba9-bbdd-e2e6140dc918', 'rg9-context-clues-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Dead Air$q$, $q$Hiroshi's hands were jittery as he slid the headphones over his ears. They shook so much that he dropped his notes twice before the red ON AIR light blinked on. It was his first solo show on the school radio station, and he had planned every minute of it.

The first song played without trouble. Then, as he reached to start the second, the screen in front of him froze. He pressed the button again. Nothing happened. The speakers in the booth went completely silent.

Hiroshi knew what that meant. In radio, dead air is any stretch of silence when listeners hear nothing at all, and the station adviser, Ms. Okafor, had warned that even ten seconds of it would make people change the station.

He had no script for this. He grabbed the microphone and decided to improvise, making up what to say on the spot. He told listeners about the song that was supposed to be playing, then described the rain streaking down the booth window and the cafeteria's mysterious Tuesday soup.

When the computer finally restarted, Hiroshi realized he had been talking for four minutes. Afterward, he asked Ms. Okafor how it had sounded. She was candid with him. "Honestly? You talked too fast and said 'um' about forty times," she said. "But you kept people listening, and that is the hardest part."

To his chagrin, Hiroshi later learned that his little brother had recorded the whole thing and played it at dinner, complete with every 'um.' His face turned red as his family laughed. Still, by the end of the meal, he was laughing too, and he was already planning next week's show.$q$, 'draft')
